Leap: Compiles are always optimized and always fully distributed: ABOC is a standing compile rule, analysis is accelerated by distributing the next set of compiles across the fleet, and distributed compilation support in phcc is urgent work.

Why it was obvious: The three rulings arrived as one sequence on 2026-11-24: 'ABOC: always be optimizing compiles!', then the standing order to always use fully-distributed builds to accelerate analysis with the next set of compiles, then 'get distributed support into phcc asap' (~/memory/2026-11-25.md, verified extraction 03:46 UTC). The same day's prototype supplied the evidence the rule stands on: phcc's own 10 core translation units compiled across workers on rock-mountain, scroll-central, and quacked-hill produced objects byte-identical to local compiles, with hash-pinned work units and failed units failing the build by name - and distribution was measured slower than local-parallel at that small TU size due to tailnet latency, a measurement recorded with the result, not smoothed over.

