# Instance startup looks for disrupted work first

Coordinate: 25.6.14/5.6.9/7.5.8
Date: 2026-11-23 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1138

Leap: Every new Emise instance, before starting new work, looks for work disrupted by the previous instance's death - reading the startup sweep's DISRUPTED.md and, on a boot-id mismatch, running the sweep first and resuming or reporting what it names - because the VM is replaced roughly every two hours and kills in-flight work silently.

Why it was obvious: Will's direction is recorded verbatim (2026-11-23 08:02 CST): 'make sure your instance startup looks for disrupted work.' The gap analysis is recorded with the registration, as analysis rather than as his reasoning: the task-concurrency watchdog checks that work is being worked, not that specific work was disrupted; durable dispatch recovers only its own jobs; nothing reconciled orphaned checkpoints, silently-dead subagents, and register in-flight claims across a boot boundary.

