# This box is never a download waypoint

Coordinate: 29.5.7/7.5.10/5.5.9
Date: 2026-11-17 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1132

Leap: The Muse VM's disk is rarely used and the box is never a download waypoint: work stays in memory or ranch-resident, transfers are hash-first and delta-only (rsync-style), shared state lives on the ranch, and bulk acquisition downloads directly on the ranch node that needs the data, coordinated by SHA-256 and phext coordinate.

Why it was obvious: Born of the 2026-11-17 I/O diagnosis Will asked for twice that evening: this cell is I/O-bound (PSI io stall ~17%; iowait up to ~63% in samples) on a slow disk shared with other tenants, crawling over a million files. The measured constraints: 7.9 GB RAM with only ~0.7 GB available at rest, and /tmp + /dev/shm are tmpfs (~800 MB each), so scratch already lands in RAM - the order bites on staging, transfer topology, and where shared state lives. Will's orders are verbatim: 23:27 CST 'attempt to rarely use the disk on this box - try to keep all work in memory and dispatch to the ranch via hashes and shared state - rsync style,' and the 23:38 CST corollary 'never download locally only to stream to the ranch, just download remotely on the ranch and coordinate via hashes and coordinates.' Downloads that terminate here for Emise's own local use are not the banned pattern; the ban is local-as-relay.

Cross-references:
- MEMORY.md: 'STANDING ORDER - rarely use this box's disk (Will, 2026-11-17 23:27 CST, verbatim)' and its 23:38 CST corollary
- AGENTS.md standing rules: the same order and corollary recorded the same evening
- ~/memory/2026-11-18.md: 'STANDING ORDER - rarely use this box's disk' section (flushed after midnight; both orders dated 2026-11-17) with the evening's disk and I/O measurements
