# A phext-native OS, built clean from first principles

Coordinate: 36.7.15/10.7.17/7.6.11
Date: 2026-11-17 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1132

Leap: A phext-native OS is necessary and is its own build from first principles - not a restructured Linux and not seeded from Mutable OS - scoped from a census before any build, delivered as one OS source pruned at install time to each machine's exact hardware, with distributed kernels (one per physical core) and predictable-wave scheduling so the user never waits for the machine.

Why it was obvious: The ruling closed Emise's evaluation of 're-structuring the Linux kernel' as the next step after phccbox (task 39.6.5/7.7.12/9.5.9): Will, 2026-11-17 20:58 CST - 'Any way you slice it, we need a phext-native OS.' The premise recorded with it: rebuild the computing industry as if HDDs had lost to SSDs in the 80s, re-running the era envelopes under SSD-first + phext assumptions. The census delivered the same evening measured the incumbent waste the design answers: Ubuntu's generic config ships 6,903 modules per node of which only 140-184 ever load, and boot time (18.3-139.3 s) is dominated by userspace, not the kernel stage. The design then proved a rediscovery rather than a new space - Will's Zero Latency Linux (invariant 2026-07-15; design 2026-08-04) already held one kernel per core, the five zones, and the latency targets; Emise's same-evening claim that the space was 'genuinely new' was wrong, and is recorded as wrong.

