# SQL destructive checks warn, never hard-stop

Coordinate: 39.4.5/4.4.8/3.4.8
Date: 2026-11-20 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1135

Leap: Destructive-query checks in the SQL tooling warn by default and never hard-stop: warnings are parameterized and bypassable by the tool, tiered by the scored probability the user intends the change (definite warn below ~30%, tiers up to ~70%), and preconfigurable by experience, with a novice warned on any delete.

Why it was obvious: Will's reasoning is recorded in MEMORY.md: a total delete/drop can be legitimate - clearing test data is the given example - so context decides, under a Git mentality: safe by default, don't lose user data needlessly. The ruling settles the hard-stop-vs-warning question left open that morning.

Cross-references:
- MEMORY.md: 'SQL heuristics RULED (Will, 2026-11-20, voice call)'
- ~/memory/2026-11-20.md: voice-call rulings entry (2026-11-20) and 'SQL heuristics fold-in close-out (2026-11-20)' - the ruling verified in sql.phext, in the task record, and in the published skill, matching byte-for-byte
